# Human-owned knobs for the Phase 0 null trader. Sourced by run_phase0.sh.
#
# RUNG LADDER — raise ONE rung at a time, each a deliberate human edit:
#   RUNG-0  DRY_RUN=1  EXECUTION=simulator   compute + log targets only
#   RUNG-1  DRY_RUN=0  EXECUTION=simulator   live shadow ledger, no orders sent
#   RUNG-2  DRY_RUN=0  EXECUTION=ibkr        real orders to the IBKR PAPER account
#                                            (requires TWS/Gateway logged in on
#                                             127.0.0.1:7497 — see config/.env)
#
# CURRENT RUNG: RUNG-2. This is the workflow's Phase 0 control experiment, so it
# is SUPPOSED to send orders — its whole purpose is to measure what real fills do
# to a book with no edge in it. Expected outcome: net P&L ≈ -(costs). If it makes
# money, the fill or accounting logic is wrong and every later result is suspect.
#
# It is a PAPER account (DUQ199038, NetLiquidation ~1.00M CAD). No real capital.

# HALTED 2026-07-31 17:02 ET, RESTORED 18:0x. Same fault as cef.env: this
# sleeve's positions.csv held CASH only while the account held the full 14-ETF
# null book at $640k gross, so the 09:35 fire would have diffed against flat and
# doubled it.
#
# This book is the harder arming case and it is now covered. null_trader shares
# HYG/JNK/LQD/VCIT/EMB/SHYG/USHY with the benchmark books, and because each book
# runs in its own process it looked like the sole owner of all of them —
# measured, arm() would have adopted 823 HYG when this sleeve owns 541, then sold
# 282 shares it never bought. Fixed two ways: arm() now reads sibling book specs
# and refuses to take the account net for any symbol another book trades, and
# scripts/ops/reconcile_attribution.py rebuilds the missing per-sleeve
# attribution from broker fills into _attribution.json (including EXPLICIT ZEROS,
# without which USHY blocked arming — this sleeve targets 0 of it while
# bench_b6_ew_credit holds 67).
#
# Verified 2026-07-31: arms clean, all 14 attributions correct.
DRY_RUN=0
EXECUTION=ibkr
# 47, added 2026-09-10. This book had NO id of its own and fell back to the
# shared broker config, so it contended with whatever else read that default.
# IB allows one session per id; the second connect evicts the first, which then
# reports a dead broker. 47 keeps every derived id clear too: capture_fills
# uses id+50 (97), preflight id+60 (107), reconcile_orders id+70 (117), none of
# which collide. Inventory: docs/INFRASTRUCTURE.md 6.2.
IBKR_CLIENT_ID=47

# PYTHON=/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3

# BOOK / BOOKS_ROOT deliberately absent (2026-09-10, prod/dev split). They used
# to pin absolute paths into ~/Desktop. Once the scheduler runs from ~/prod, an
# absolute dev path here would make the manual `ops/schedule/run_*.sh` wrapper
# write to a DIFFERENT book than the scheduled job does -- two ledgers claiming
# the same account. The wrappers already default them from their own location
# (REPO="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/../.." && pwd)"), and the launchd
# entry point derives them from its own REPO, so every path now follows the tree
# the code is running from. Set them only to point a one-off run somewhere
# unusual, and unset them again afterwards.
